                "reviews": [
                    {
                        "recommendationid": "238283581",
                        "author": {
                            "steamid": "76561198024726928",
                            "num_reviews": 2,
                            "playtime_forever": 21595,
                            "playtime_last_two_weeks": 0,
                            "playtime_at_review": 21595,
                            "last_played": 1780106480
                        },
                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "I'm no authority but I've played all of them: 13 and 13-2 are my favorite Final Fantasy games; I prefer 13-2.  The story is compelling and the gameplay holds up extraordinarily well.  Throughout and even into very late post game, you are still chasing meaningful improvement and can find fun and rewarding challenges.  The visuals hold up well, the environments are diverse and interesting, the soundtrack is tremendous, and the game compels you to satisfyingly revisit and explore locations many times for a number of reasons.  \n\nThe level based stat distribution 'crystarium' is my least favorite version of this sort of thing in any FF game.  If you are a min maxing type A jrpg player, you will need to use a guide full stop.  Making a 'wrong' choice is hardly fatal, but it is annoying and there is no recovery.  For a game of this nature, there is a modestly high volume of grinding, fetch quests, and other nonsense which may turn many players off, but if you've ever intentionally bred a pokemon you'll probably get it and likely buy in.\n\nReserved praise aside, this release for PC on Steam is an unmitigated embarrassment.  The staff at Square Enix should be ashamed of themselves to ask for money for this port.  This game has been sold to actual people for actual dollars on this platform for, at the time of writing, almost twelve years.  Out of the box, it has not been a functional product that entire time.  I revisit this game every few years and love it; several hundred hours on Steam alone to prove it out.  Frustratingly, however, every re-install I resent needing to relearn third party modding so that I can achieve foundational gameplay experiences such as: see through in-game weather whose native transparency is broken or avoid reliable crashing due to the native 2GB RAM limitation.\n\nIn the last year or so, there has been some chatter online regarding revisiting the 13 1-3 games in pursuit of remakes/remaster, who knows.  I would appreciate, before the game is reworked from the ground up and resold for seventy dollars per chapter, maybe some work can be applied to this release which has been sold for over a decade as a broken toy.\n\nListen up.  Moms are tough.",
